Sp. ''antigüedad'' = Pg. ''antiguidade'' = It. ''antichità'', < L. ''antiquita(t-)s'', < ''antiquus'': see ''antique''.] '''1.''' The quality of being ancient; ancientness; great age: as, a family of great ''antiquity''.
{{smaller block|This ring is valuable for its ''antiquity''. {{right|''Johnson.''}}
Is not your voice broken? your wind short? . . . and every part about you blasted with ''antiquity?''
{{right|''Shak.'', 2 Hen. IV., i. 2.}}}}
'''2.''' Ancient times; former ages; times long since past: as, Demosthenes was the most eloquent orator of ''antiquity''.
{{smaller block|Nor even so remotely among the mossy centuries did it pause, but strayed onward into that gray ''antiquity'' of which there is no token left save its cavernous tombs, etc.
{{right|''Hawthorne'', Marble Faun.}}}}
'''3.''' The ancients collectively; the people of ancient times.
{{smaller block|He lives with ''antiquity'' and posterity; with ''antiquity'', in the sweet communion of studious retirement; and with posterity, in the generous aspirings after future renown.
{{right|''Irving'', Sketch-Book, p. 29.}}
That such pillars were raised by Seth all ''antiquity'' has avowed. {{right|''Sir W. Raleigh.''}}}}
'''4.''' An old person. [Humorous.]
{{smaller block|You are a shrewd ''antiquity'', neighbour Clench.
{{right|''B. Jonson.''}}}}
'''5.''' That which is ancient, or belongs to old or ancient times; something left by or peculiar to the ancients: generally in the plural: as, Greek or Egyptian ''antiquities''.
{{smaller block|The lectures will have for a common object the history and ''antiquities'' of the country. {{right|''Everett'', Orations, II. 111.}}}}

