'''antiracer''' (an-ti-rā′sėr), ''n.'' [< ''anti-'' + ''race''<sup>1</sup> + ''-er''<sup>1</sup>.] A device for preventing the racing of the screw of a marine propeller when the vessel pitches so as to throw it out of the water.

'''antirachitic''' (an″ti-ra-kit′ik), ''a.'' [< ''anti-'' + ''rachitic.''] Tending to cure rachitis or rickets.

'''antiremonstrant''' (an″ti-rē-mon′strant), ''n.'' [< ''anti-'' + ''remonstrant.''] One opposed to remonstrance or to those who remonstrate. {{smaller|Specifically (with a capital), one of that party in the Dutch Calvinistic Church which opposed the Remonstrants or Arminians. They are also called ''Counter-remonstrants.'' See ''remonstrant.''}}

'''antirent''' (an-ti-rent′), ''a.'' [< ''anti-'' + ''rent.''] Opposed to the payment of rent; opposed, on theoretical grounds, to the exaction of rent for land, etc.: as, ''antirent'' doctrines.—'''Antirent party''', {{smaller|a social and political organization which resisted (1839 to about 1849) the collection of rent on certain great manorial estates in the State of New York.}}

'''antirenter''' (an-ti-ren′tėr), ''n.'' [< ''antirent'' + ''-er''<sup>1</sup>.] A person opposed to the payment of rent; specifically, a member of the Antirent party.

'''Antirrhinum''' (an-ti-rī′num), ''n.'' [NL., < Gr. ἀντί, corresponding to, like, + ῥίς, ῥίν, nose.] A genus of herbs, natural order ''Scrophulariaceæ'', natives of the warmer parts of the old world and North America. {{smaller|The flowers of most of the species bear a resemblance to an animal’s snout; hence the name. The snapdragon, ''A. majus'', is a familiar garden-plant, with showy flowers, from the Mediterranean. The Mexi-}}

